{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue128;}{\fonttbl{\f0
\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}}\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\fs28\b FM2: Frequency 
Modulation (FM) with Differentiator Demodulator.}}
.TXT 3 0 16323 0 0
Cg a73.000000,73.000000,73
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue128;}{\fonttbl{\f0
\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}}\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard by\par \par Vinh Bui, 
Anuj Mehta, Vincent Park, Jim Roberts, Satish Yelamanchili\par }
.TXT 10 6 17136 0 0
Cg a82.000000,82.000000,1273
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue128;}{\fonttbl{\f0
\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}}\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\b\ul Introduction\par 
\par }In this document we test two FM system types.  The two systems 
are equal in design,\par except that the first uses a Difference 
Method for the differentiator in the receiver, while the\par second 
system uses Fast Convolution with an ideal differentiator.\par \par 
The Difference Method is quite simple.  The first derivative is 
approximated by subtracting\par a discrete data point (at n) from the 
next discrete data point (at n+1).  Naturally this system\par is 
non-causal, and a delay is introduced.  As it stands, the Discrete 
Difference Method only\par approximates the actual derivative; as the 
sampling period decreases to zero, the Discrete \par Difference Method 
should approach the true derivative.\par \par Using the Convolution 
Method, we first find the DFT of the received signal.  Secondly, we
\par multiply that DFT by the ideal differentiator.  Then the IDFT is 
calculated.  The resulting\par signal undergoes processing and 
envolope detection to recover the original message (as\par does the 
signal from the Discrete Difference Method).\par \par In this first 
document, we compare the two methods under "bare-bones" conditions, 
i.e.,\par we leave out the hard-limiter in the receiver since it seems 
to add its own set of \par complications.  The second document 
(FM2LIM.MCD) deals with the hard-limiter case.\par \par \par \par }
.TXT 52 -6 17139 0 0
