{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue128;}{\fonttbl{\f0
\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}}\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\b\ul Conclusion}\par 
\par The two methods seem to produce roughly equal results.  When 
noise is low, the errors\par between methods are very similar at each 
corresponding noise factor.  When the noise\par factor exceeds 0.001, 
both methods "lose"  the original message.  Considering this \par 
information, the difference method may be a better choice since it is 
more efficient with\par regard to calculation intensity, even though 
the convolution method offers a slightly \par better noise tolerance 
at a noise factor below 0.001.  Clearly, both systems need\par 
improvement, and that is the subject of the second document, 
FM2LIM.MCD.  \par \par Also, we haven't taken into account the 
performance differences that might occur when the sampling rate 
varies.  Although we are out of time for this project, it would be 
interesting to test each system's tolerance under various sampling rates.}
